Main PTY management code lives in `/home/namastex/prod/genie-desktop/src/main/services/ptyManager.ts`, wired to IPC in `/home/namastex/prod/genie-desktop/src/main/ipc/ptyIpc.ts`, with a thin Claude adapter in `/home/namastex/prod/genie-desktop/src/main/agents/claude/index.ts`. It uses `node-pty` (lazy-required). The renderer-side session wrapper is `/home/namastex/prod/genie-desktop/src/renderer/terminal/TerminalSessionManager.ts`.

## 1. PTY session registry (Map of id -> pty process)

`src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:21-38`

```ts
interface PtyRecord {
  proc: any; // IPty from node-pty
  cwd: string;
  isDirectSpawn: boolean;
  owner: WebContents | null;
  kind: PtyKind;
  taskId: string | null;
  featureId: string | null;
  mirror: TerminalMirror | null;
}

const ptys = new Map<string, PtyRecord>();
```

This module-level `Map<string, PtyRecord>` is the single registry for every kind of PTY (`agent`, `shell`, `tui`, `service` — `PtyKind` declared at line 19). It's populated by three spawn functions: `startDirectPty` (line 405, sets record at line 522), `startPty` (line 563, sets record at line 645), and `startCommandPty` (line 860, sets record at line 906). Lookups/iterations happen throughout the file (`hasPty` line 182-184, `writePty` line 685-690, `resizePty` line 695-705, `listForTask` line 840-852, `killPtyInternal` line 777-782, `killAll` line 808-818, `killByOwner` line 825-832).

Test-only escape hatches for seeding/clearing the map directly: `__testReset` (line 936-942) and `__registerForTest` (line 944-958).

## 2. Terminal resize -> PTY

- IPC handler (fire-and-forget, since resize is high frequency): `src/main/ipc/ptyIpc.ts:103-111`
```ts
ipcMain.on('pty:resize', (_event, args: { id: string; cols: number; rows: number }) => {
  const v = parseArgsSafe(
    'pty:resize',
    z.looseObject({ id: z.string(), cols: z.number(), rows: z.number() }),
    args,
  );
  if (v === undefined) return;
  resizePty(args.id, args.cols, args.rows);
});
```

- Implementation: `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:692-705`
```ts
/**
 * Resize a PTY.
 */
export function resizePty(id: string, cols: number, rows: number): void {
  const record = ptys.get(id);
  if (record) {
    record.mirror?.resize(cols, rows);
    try {
      record.proc.resize(cols, rows);
    } catch {
      // EBADF can happen during transitions
    }
  }
}
```
The `record.proc.resize(cols, rows)` call (line 700) is the actual node-pty resize; it's wrapped in try/catch because an EBADF can occur mid-transition (e.g., PTY dying).

- Renderer trigger: `src/renderer/terminal/TerminalSessionManager.ts:978-1003` (`fitTerminal()`), which calls `window.electronAPI.ptyResize({ id: this.id, cols, rows: dims.rows })` at line 1002, skipping redundant resizes when cols/rows are unchanged (lines 998-999) to avoid unnecessary SIGWINCH-driven prompt redraws. Also used for theme-driven SIGWINCH forcing at lines 936-945 (`setTerminalTheme`), sending `rows+1` then `rows` 50ms apart to force the TUI to repaint.

## 3. PTY shutdown / kill logic

Graceful-kill core with the timeout: `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:707-763`

```ts
// Grace window for a SIGTERM'd child to flush and exit before we force SIGKILL.
const GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS = 3000;

/**
 * Gracefully terminate a pty's child process: send SIGTERM so it can flush and
 * exit cleanly, then escalate to SIGKILL only if it overstays the grace window.
 * Resolves once the process is gone (or was already dead).
 *
 * node-pty's bare `kill()` sends SIGHUP, which Claude Code does not trap — so
 * its in-memory session tail (the last several turns) was lost on every
 * refresh/quit, and no `--resume`/`--continue` could recover what never
 * reached the jsonl. SIGTERM + a wait gives Claude the chance to persist first.
 */
...
function gracefulKillProc(proc: KillableProc, timeoutMs = GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
    ...
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      try {
        proc.kill('SIGKILL');
      } catch {
        // already dead
      }
      finish();
    }, timeoutMs);
    try {
      proc.kill('SIGTERM');
    } catch {
      // already dead — nothing to wait for
      finish();
    }
  });
}
```

- Exact timeout value: **`GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS = 3000`** (3 seconds), defined at `ptyManager.ts:708`.
- Sequence: `proc.kill('SIGTERM')` immediately (line 757); if the process's `onExit` doesn't fire within 3000ms, `proc.kill('SIGKILL')` is issued (line 750) and the promise resolves either way (`finish()`).
- `teardownRecord` (lines 767-775) removes the record from the `ptys` map, unregisters from `activityMonitor`/`remoteControlService`/`contextUsageService`, and persists+disposes the terminal mirror *before* killing, so the snapshot exists even if kill hangs.
- Callers:
  - `killPtyInternal` (777-782) / `killPty` (789-791, fire-and-forget) / `killPtyAwait` (799-801, awaited — used by renderer restart/reattach flows to avoid a two-writer race on the session jsonl, see `TerminalSessionManager.ts:538-542` and `745`).
  - `killAll()` (808-818) — used on app quit; awaits all children's graceful exit in parallel (bounded by ~one grace window, not the sum).
  - `killByOwner()` (825-832) — fire-and-forget graceful kill of all PTYs owned by a closing window.
- IPC exposure: `src/main/ipc/ptyIpc.ts:113-117` (`pty:kill`, fire-and-forget) and `:122-130` (`pty:kill-await`, awaited).
- Note on plain `.kill()` (non-graceful) usage: `startDirectPty` at `ptyManager.ts:429-436` calls `existing.proc.kill()` with no signal argument (default SIGHUP per node-pty) when replacing a mismatched shell-vs-agent PTY on reattach — this bypasses the graceful path deliberately since it's an immediate replace, not a "let it flush" kill.

## 4. Spawn environment specifics

The main spawn env builder is `buildDirectEnv` — `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:231-340` (used for Claude/agent PTYs via the adapter). Highlights with line citations:

- **Base env**: minimal by default; only inherits full `process.env` if `syncShellEnv` is enabled by the user (lines 232-235, toggle via `setSyncShellEnv` line 190-192).
- **PATH**: merges in the Dash-managed `rtk` binary directory ahead of the inherited `PATH` (lines 238-242, helper `prependUnique` at 348-353).
- **Custom env set**: `env.TERM_PROGRAM = 'dash'`, `env.HOME = os.homedir()`, `env.PATH = mergedPath`, `env.COLORFGBG = isDark ? '15;0' : '0;15'` (lines 244-252) — the `COLORFGBG` value follows the rxvt convention to tell CLI apps the terminal background lightness.
- **Windows branch** (lines 254-277): sets `USERNAME`, and copies through `APPDATA`, `LOCALAPPDATA`, `USERPROFILE`, `TEMP`, `TMP`, `SystemRoot`/`SYSTEMROOT` (both casings — cmd.exe vs PowerShell), `SystemDrive`, `WINDIR`, `COMSPEC`, `PATHEXT`, `COMPUTERNAME`, `USERDOMAIN`, `ProgramFiles`.
- **Non-Windows branch** (lines 278-282): `env.TERM = 'xterm-256color'`, `env.COLORTERM = 'truecolor'`, `env.USER = os.userInfo().username`.
- **Auth passthrough** when not doing full syncShellEnv (lines 284-303): `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY`/`NO_PROXY` and lowercase variants.
- **User-configured env vars** from settings merged in, guarded against a reserved-key set (lines 305-311); `RESERVED_ENV_KEYS` is defined at lines 127-138 (`PATH`, `HOME`, `USER`, `TERM`, `COLORTERM`, `TERM_PROGRAM`, `COLORFGBG`, `DASH_HOOK_PORT`).
- **Per-task port env vars** merged from `WorkspacePortsRuntime.getEnvForWorktree(cwd)` (lines 313-324) — e.g. `FRONTEND_PORT`.
- **`env.CLAUDE_CODE_NO_FLICKER = '1'`** (line 327) — disables Claude Code's own viewport scrolling since Dash renders its own terminal viewport (referenced again in `TerminalSessionManager.ts:964-966`).
- **`env.DASH_HOOK_PORT`** set last (lines 329-337) to the live `HookServer` port, only if the server is actually bound (`hookServer.port !== 0`), so hooks curl back into Dash; absent when a session was launched outside Dash so the guarded curl no-ops.
- **cwd**: PTY cwd is simply `spawnPlan.cwd`, forwarded straight from the adapter's `buildSpawn` (`src/main/agents/claude/index.ts:38-54`, returns `{ command, args: spawnArgs, env, cwd: ctx.cwd }`), itself just `ctx.cwd` passed in from `startDirectPty`'s `options.cwd` (`ptyManager.ts:486, 507`).
- **Shell selection** (Windows binary invocation quirk): `src/main/agents/claude/index.ts:48-51` — on Windows, `claude` is a `.cmd` shim so it's invoked via `cmd.exe /c <binary> <args>` rather than directly.

The actual `pty.spawn(...)` call sites:
- Agent/Claude PTY: `ptyManager.ts:502-509`
```ts
const pty = getPty();
const proc = pty.spawn(spawnPlan.command, spawnPlan.args, {
  name: 'xterm-256color',
  cols: options.cols,
  rows: options.rows,
  cwd: spawnPlan.cwd,
  env: spawnPlan.env,
});
```
- Interactive shell fallback PTY: `ptyManager.ts:580-623` — shell selection is `isWin ? 'powershell.exe' : process.env.SHELL || '/bin/bash'` (line 583); uses interactive-not-login flags (`-i` on POSIX, `-NoLogo` on Windows, lines 586-588, with a comment on why login files are skipped for startup speed); strips Electron artifacts `ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE`/`ELECTRON_NO_ATTACH_CONSOLE` from the inherited env (lines 591-594); sets `TERM_PROGRAM = 'Apple_Terminal'` on macOS to enable zsh OSC 7 cwd reporting (lines 598-600); and for zsh specifically sets `env.ZDOTDIR = ensureShellConfig()` (line 604) to inject a custom Dash prompt — implemented in `src/main/services/ptyShellConfig.ts:60-86` (writes `.zshenv`/`.zprofile`/`.zshrc`/`.zlogin`/`prompt.zsh` under `userData/shell`, chaining to the user's real dotfiles then restoring `ZDOTDIR=$HOME` and layering Dash's minimal `%1~` / `$` prompt on top — see `SHELL_ZSHRC` at `ptyShellConfig.ts:27-33` and `SHELL_PROMPT` at lines 39-51).
- `startCommandPty` (side-car TUI/service processes, e.g. ports onboarding): `ptyManager.ts:886-893` — spawns with `env: { ...process.env, ...(options.env ?? {}) }`, i.e. full inherited env plus caller-supplied overrides, no filtering.

I did not find any reference to `CHOKIDAR_USEPOLLING` anywhere in the tracked source tree (`grep -rn "CHOKIDAR_USEPOLLING"` over non-`node_modules`/`dist` returned nothing), and I was not able to search git commit history (the `Bash` tool denied `git log` invocations in this session), so I can't confirm or deny that variable's presence in history — flagging this as unverified rather than reporting a guess.

