Confirmed against the source. Here's the rundown:

**1. Session registry** — `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:38`
```ts
const ptys = new Map<string, PtyRecord>();
```
A module-level map keyed by session id, storing `{ proc, cwd, isDirectSpawn, owner, kind, taskId, featureId, mirror }` (`ptyManager.ts:21-30`). Populated by `startDirectPty` (~405-522), `startPty` (~563-645), and `startCommandPty` (~860-906).

**2. Resize** — `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:695-705`, called from the renderer via `pty:resize` IPC (`src/main/ipc/ptyIpc.ts:103-111`, a fire-and-forget `ipcMain.on`):
```ts
export function resizePty(id: string, cols: number, rows: number): void {
  const record = ptys.get(id);
  if (record) {
    record.mirror?.resize(cols, rows);
    try {
      record.proc.resize(cols, rows);   // ptyManager.ts:700
    } catch {
      // EBADF can happen during transitions
    }
  }
}
```
Triggered from `TerminalSessionManager.ts:978-1003` (`fitTerminal()`), which skips the IPC call if cols/rows haven't changed.

**3. Shutdown & timeout** — `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:708` defines the grace window:
```ts
const GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS = 3000; // 3 seconds
```
`gracefulKillProc` (`ptyManager.ts:727` onward) sends `SIGTERM` first, starts a `setTimeout(..., 3000)`, and escalates to `SIGKILL` if the process hasn't exited by then. A code comment explains why: node-pty's bare `.kill()` sends `SIGHUP`, which Claude Code doesn't trap, so the in-memory session tail was being lost on refresh/quit — SIGTERM gives it a chance to flush to the jsonl first. Exposed via IPC as `pty:kill` (fire-and-forget) and `pty:kill-await` (awaited) in `ptyIpc.ts:113-130`. One exception: `startDirectPty` (~429-436) calls a plain `.kill()` (default SIGHUP) when replacing a mismatched shell-vs-agent PTY on reattach — an intentional bypass since it's an immediate swap, not a graceful stop.

**4. Spawn environment** — built in `buildDirectEnv` (`ptyManager.ts:231-340`), notable specifics:
- Starts minimal (not full `process.env`) unless the user enables "sync shell env"; PATH is prepended with Dash's managed `rtk` binary dir (~238-242).
- Sets `TERM_PROGRAM='dash'`, `HOME`, and `COLORFGBG` encoding light/dark theme (rxvt convention) at ~244-252.
- Non-Windows: `TERM='xterm-256color'`, `COLORTERM='truecolor'`, `USER` (~278-282). Windows branch carries through the usual `APPDATA`/`SystemRoot`/`COMSPEC`/etc. (~254-277).
- Selectively passes through auth/proxy vars (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `GH_TOKEN`, `HTTP_PROXY`, ...) when not fully syncing shell env (~284-303).
- Merges user-configured env vars from settings, guarded by a `RESERVED_ENV_KEYS` set (~127-138, 305-311) so users can't clobber `PATH`/`TERM`/etc.
- Sets `CLAUDE_CODE_NO_FLICKER='1'` (line 327) so Claude Code doesn't do its own viewport scrolling, since Dash renders the terminal viewport itself.
- Sets `DASH_HOOK_PORT` last (~329-337) to the live `HookServer` port so Claude Code hooks can curl back into the app.
- The interactive-shell fallback path (`ptyManager.ts:580-623`) is more shell-specific: uses `-i` (POSIX) / `-NoLogo` (Windows) to skip slow login-file loading, strips Electron env artifacts, sets `TERM_PROGRAM='Apple_Terminal'` on macOS for zsh OSC 7 cwd reporting, and for zsh sets `ZDOTDIR` to a Dash-managed dir (`ptyShellConfig.ts:60-86`) that chains to the user's real dotfiles then layers a custom minimal prompt.

Note: `CHOKIDAR_USEPOLLING` doesn't appear anywhere in the current source tree — the agent couldn't run `git log` in this session to check history, so that's unverified, not confirmed absent.

